---
id: PY-008
type: rule
family: python-reliability
title: eval() / exec() / compile() called with a dynamic argument
version: 1

severity: error
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 8

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.py]
allowlist: ['**/test_*.py', '**/*_test.py', '**/tests/**', '**/conftest.py', '**/explain/**']

detection:
  type: regex
  # RE2 has no lookahead. Static: bare quotes and r/b/u/rb prefixes.
  # Dynamic: identifier then `,` `)` `(` `.` `[`, or an f-string (f-strings
  # interpolate). Do not subtract prefix letters from the identifier class.
  pattern: '\b(eval|exec|compile)\s*\(\s*(?:[fF][''"]|[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\s*[,().\[])'

related: [AP-008, PY-009]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

Don't call `eval()`, `exec()`, or `compile(..., "exec")` with a dynamic
argument. An identifier as the first argument means the string being executed
is not a static literal and may be attacker-controlled at runtime.

Replace with a structured dispatch — a dict of callables keyed on the input, a
pydantic / attrs schema for data validation, or a restricted interpreter
(`ast.literal_eval` for literals only, RestrictedPython, a subprocess with a
clean env) if you genuinely need to run untrusted code.

If the argument is a string literal you control end-to-end (e.g. `eval("1 + 1")`
or a raw/bytes/unicode prefix such as `re.compile(r'^\d+$')`,
`compile(rb"...", "<s>", "exec")`, `eval(u"1")`), the rule skips you. f-strings
are not treated as static — they can interpolate run-time values.

## Why It's Harmful

`eval()`, `exec()`, and `compile()` turn a run-time string into code in the
process's full privilege. When the first argument is an identifier, static
analysis cannot see the payload — it may be attacker-controlled.

Raw, bytes, and unicode prefixes (`r`, `b`, `u`, `rb`) are treated as static,
the same as a bare quote. Forms such as `re.compile(r'^\d+$')`,
`compile(rb"...", "<s>", "exec")`, and `eval(u"1")` do not fire. f-strings
and true dynamic arguments (`eval(user_input)`, `eval(user_input.strip())`,
`eval(user_input[0])`, `exec(payload)`, `compile(src, '<str>', 'exec')`)
still do.
